    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    <persName key="Q31956567">Norah C. James</persName> forwards letter from <persName key="Q4695257">Mr. Ahmed Ali</persName> concerning Indian edition  
                    of <title>Twilight in Delhi</title> to <persName key="Q703935">Leonard Woolf</persName>, asking for his permission to publish it. The typescript 
                    letter is signed by <persName key="Q31956567">James</persName>. The letter contains a handwritten note by <persName key="Q703935">Woolf</persName> to 
                    <persName key="Q1563198">John Lehmann</persName> about the possibility of publishing the Indian edition.
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>
        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <dateline>
                        <placeName>
                            6, Henrietta Street, Covent Garden, <placeName>London</placeName>, W.C.2
                        </placeName>
                        <date when="1944-03-30">
                            30 March 1944
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                    <address>
                        <addrLine>
                            <persName key="Q703935">Leonard <choice><sic>Wolfe</sic><corr>Woolf</corr></choice></persName> 
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            Messrs <orgName key="Q1623362">Hogarth Press</orgName>
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            Pixmore Avenue <choice><sic>LETCHWORT H</sic><corr>LETCHWORTH</corr></choice> Herts
                        </addrLine>
                    </address>
                    <salute>
                        Dear <persName key="Q703935">Mr. <choice><sic>Wolfe</sic><corr>Woolf</corr></choice></persName>,
                    </salute>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <add place="Letchworth" type="handwriting">
                        <persName key="Q1563198">J.L.</persName>
                        I presume we haven’t the paper for this?
                        If that is so, I think we must get permission
                        <persName key="Q703935">LW</persName>
                    </add>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <persName key="Q18936256">Mrs. Spencer Curtis Brown</persName> is away from the office at </lb>the moment 
                    so I am writing to you in her absence. We <lb/>have received an airmail from <persName key="Q4695257">Mr. Ahmed Ali</persName> 
                    in which he <lb/>says: <pb/>“Now that <title>TWILIGHT IN DELHI</title> has been included in <lb/>the list of books 
                    recommended to the British soldiers <lb/>it should do very well indeed. This list, I find <lb/>will be distributed 
                    to all the British soldiers <lb/>in this country (<placeName>India</placeName>) or coming to it, and will <lb/>
                    become operative in another couple of months. A <lb/>cheap edition therefore will be so much better and <lb/>
                    an Indian edition, because easily available in this <lb/>country will be better still. I also discovered <lb/>
                    that the novel won good points in a competition held <lb/>by two papers - the <title>Illustrated Weekly of India</title> 
                    and <lb/>the <title>B<del>i</del><add>o</add>mbay Chronicle</title>. It was within the first twenty <lb/>books on <placeName>India</placeName> - which 
                    included the classics and <lb/>philosophies and other books in a Best Books com<lb/>petition on <placeName>India</placeName> run by 
                    <persName key="Q80900">Pearl Buck</persName>. <title>TWILIGHT</title> was <lb/>within the first ten books. 
                    Two Indian publishers <lb/>have been asking me to give them permission to bring <lb/>out an edition immediately, 
                    of 3000 copies.” <pb/>He is most anxious to have your permission for this <lb/>Indian edition to be brought 
                    out and I wonder, if, in the <lb/>circumstances, you would be willing to give your consent.
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours very truly,
                    </salute>
                    <signed>
                    <seg>
                        <persName key="Q31956567">Norah C. James</persName> (In the absence of <persName key="Q18936256">Mrs. <del>v</del><add>C</add>urtis Brown</persName>)
                    </seg>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
